# Process environment for the Rust Stado coordinator.
#
# Provider choice and backing-store locators intentionally do not live here.
# Install deploy/azure/stado.config.json at the path below and keep Azure
# primary, S3 backup, managed identity, release channel and agent grant in
# that single deployment config.
STADO_CONFIG=$HOME/.stado/config.json

# Coordinator/dashboard uses consumer stado-control-plane with owner-only grant
# ~/.stado/control-plane-skarbiec-token. Product object authentication instead
# uses consumer stado-object-api-verifier with the distinct owner-only grant
# ~/.stado/stado-object-api-verifier-skarbiec-token. That verifier can list/read
# exactly the namespace items in object_api.namespaces; every item exposes only
# field token. Missing, extra, duplicate, or empty credentials block startup.
# Machine and host-health routes use only stado-machine-api/token and
# stado-host-health-api/token. Managed-service routes instead use the exact
# per-deployer items in service_api.deployers; there is no global service token.

# Host writers resolve their route-only bearer directly from Skarbiec using:
#   STADO_HOST_HEALTH_API_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#   STADO_HOST_HEALTH_SKARBIEC_URL=https://skarbiec.wisent.com
#   STADO_HOST_HEALTH_SKARBIEC_CONSUMER=stado-host-health-beacon
#   STADO_HOST_HEALTH_SKARBIEC_TOKEN_FILE=$HOME/.stado/host-health-beacon-skarbiec-token
# The owner-only grant may read only stado-host-health-api. No bearer is
# projected into an environment variable or persisted outside Skarbiec.
# The fenced Azure profile declares the exact provider-neutral application
# items and item#field projections for the newly scoped
# stado-azure-workload-agent consumer. Provision its distinct owner-only token
# file before enabling Azure. The revoked legacy stado-azure-agent grant and
# stado-gcp/stado-azure/stado-aws provider items must not be recreated in any
# workload grant; provider adapters use only their managed identities.

# Endpoint contract for consuming deployments. The public base is non-secret;
# trusted-service credentials stay only in the consuming server deployment.
# Provision each field through an owner-only, non-rendering Skarbiec projection,
# never command output, logs, source, job JSON or VM customData:
#   STADO_API_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#   STADO_API_TOKEN=
#   STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN=
#   STADO_SERVICE_API_TOKEN=
# STADO_API_TOKEN must come from the caller namespace's dedicated mapped item
# (normally <namespace>-object-api/token; wisent-backend uses its exact
# wisent-backend-object-client/token contract). It authorizes no other
# namespace. Every product uses finite top-level directory prefixes or exact
# top-level object keys plus a per-namespace action allowlist; no active product
# has namespace-root object access.
# STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN authorizes exactly machine submit/status/cancel.
# STADO_SERVICE_API_TOKEN must come from the caller's exact mapped deployer item
# and authorizes only that map's service names and status/restart actions.
# The host-health token authorizes only PUT /api/host-health and cannot read
# dashboard data, submit machines, control services, or access product objects.
# No token family falls through to another bearer or Supabase.
#
# Public software release readers use the dedicated immutable route:
#   https://stado.wisent.com/api/release/object?uri=<urlencoded-stado://releases/product/path>
# Runtime/bootstrap consumers must also receive the public origin and exact
# immutable coordinates:
#   STADO_API_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#   STADO_RELEASE_VERSION=<exact-immutable-version>
#   STADO_RELEASE_PLATFORM=<exact-release-platform>
# That route accepts GET only and refuses every namespace except `releases`.
# Authenticated publishers receive only their exact
# <product>-release-publisher/token and may create objects with if_absent under
# stado://releases/<product>/; overwrite, delete, and cross-prefix access fail.
# Stado verifies those tokens through the distinct stado-release-api-verifier
# grant. Product object bearers never authorize release requests. User and
# product objects remain behind authenticated /api/object or a signed proxy.
# Never ship any STADO_* bearer to browsers.

# External prerequisites intentionally not provisioned by Stado:
# - Azure storage account and private stado container with the release tree.
# - User-assigned managed identity with Blob Data Contributor and VM
#   self-delete rights, attached through azure.vm_identity_id.
# - Per-region network resources and approved GPU quota.
# - A pre-provisioned cross-provider replica reachable through Stado.
# - Coordinator and consumer-specific Skarbiec grants described above.
# - TLS DNS/reverse proxy and a self-hosted control-plane runner.
